{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789372337,"nonce":"6AYv6f+iEUqVWH1uy7NvSoxSMPE=","title":"Python: `+` on an instance through a dynamic value read the operands as lists","message":"class Vec3:\n        def __init__(self, x, y, z): ...\n        def __add__(self, other): return Vec3(...)\n    def main():\n        acc = Vec3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)\n        acc = acc + a\n        acc = acc + a\n\nsegfaults. Inference typed `acc + a` as Object (the operator on a class\nwas typed by the numeric rules, not by `__add__`'s return type), so\n`acc` became dynamic and the second `+` went to `zb_any_arith`, whose\ncustom-category arm knows tuples and lists only: two instances were\nconcatenated as lists.\n\nFix: the type of `left op right` with `left` an instance is what the\nclass's operator method returns; `zb_any_arith` hands any operand that\nis an instance to a `zb_hook_instance_arith(code, a, b)` the frontend\ngenerates, which dispatches on the left instance's class to its method\nfor the operation and raises TypeError for anything else; and the\noperator methods' parameters are inferred from the operator sites on\nthe class, as a closed function's are. The dynamic hook reads the right\noperand back as the inferred type, so an operator method written to\ntake several operand types of which only one appears at the class's\noperator sites raises TypeError from the dynamic path where Python\nwould have run the method.\n\nbench_op_overload.py: 8.55 s to 0.50 s.","files":null}]}